; ======================================================================
;  hw_con.pi4 - the Pi 4 backend for the HwCon* seam.
;
;  THE PAYLOAD OWNS THE PIXELS AND ANVIL OWNS THE DEVICE. This file is the
;  one place that knows this board draws into a mailbox-allocated linear
;  framebuffer at 32 bits per pixel; Anvil/Hal/abi.pbi above it knows only
;  rectangles, text runs and a tier code.
;
;  It is thin over RaspberryPi4/Lib/display.pi4 (the surface: base, pitch,
;  geometry) and RaspberryPi4/Board/display.pi4 (the smooth-text
;  rasteriser and its three fonts). It re-drives nothing - the same
;  discipline hw_usb.pi4 states for the xHCI stack.
;
;  INCLUDE ORDER: after RaspberryPi4/Lib/display.pi4,
;  RaspberryPi4/Monitor/anvil_fonts.pi4 and RaspberryPi4/Board/display.pi4
;  (DrawSmoothText, SmoothTextWidth), and before Anvil/Hal/abi.pbi, which
;  calls this. House rule 10: this file includes nothing itself.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHY THE PIXELS ARE WRITTEN BY A PLAIN LOOP AND NOT BY THE DMA ENGINE
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The monitor's own console scrolls with DMA channel 0 and RunAt takes
;  that channel back on every return, because "a payload must not leave a
;  bus master running underneath the monitor" (cache.pi4). A payload
;  filling rectangles through the same channel would be the mirror of that
;  problem - two owners, one engine, no agreement - and the monitor is not
;  running to arbitrate while the payload has the machine.
;
;  A processor loop needs no arbitration, cannot be left running, and is
;  fast enough for a UI that redraws rectangles rather than whole screens.
;  HwConTier() reports #HW_TIER_CPU honestly rather than claiming a tier
;  this path does not use, so a payload that measures its frame cost knows
;  what it measured.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE ONE HONEST LIMITATION, SAID OUT LOUD RATHER THAN DISCOVERED
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THIS BOARD HAS THREE FONTS AND ONE SIZE. anvil_fonts.pi4 carries sans,
;  serif and condensed, each rasterised at a single line height (57, 58
;  and 57 pixels). The ABI's SvcText takes a pixel height because a UI
;  wants one; this backend cannot honour it, so HwConTextHeight() reports
;  the height the board actually draws at and a payload that lays out
;  against the number it asked for rather than the number it got will draw
;  outside its own boxes.
;
;  Scaled or multi-size text is real work (a second rasteriser, or a
;  distance field) and it is not Phase A. Reporting the truth costs one
;  procedure; pretending would cost somebody an afternoon in front of a
;  screen wondering why the rows overlap.
; ======================================================================

; The tier vocabulary. In Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi's portable set, restated here
; only in the comment: #HW_TIER_V3D / _DMA / _CPU.

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Geometry. Straight from the surface the mailbox allocated.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwConWidth()
  ProcedureReturn DisplayWidth()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwConHeight()
  ProcedureReturn DisplayHeight()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwConTier()
  ProcedureReturn #HW_TIER_CPU
EndProcedure

; The single line height this board's fonts are rasterised at. See the
; limitation note above.
Procedure.i HwConTextHeight()
  ProcedureReturn #FONT_sans_LINEH
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwConFrameBegin / HwConFrameEnd
;
;  THERE IS NO BACK BUFFER ON THIS PATH AND THE SEAM SAYS SO. The
;  monitor's V3D console can double-buffer; this direct path draws
;  straight into the visible surface, so Begin only checks that a surface
;  exists and End is where the cost is measured.
;
;  Begin returns 1 when there is a surface to draw on, 0 when there is
;  not - which on this board means the mailbox never gave the monitor a
;  framebuffer, and a payload that drew anyway would be writing to
;  address 0.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwConFrameBegin()
  If DisplayReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If DisplayBase() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwConFrameEnd()
  ; NOTHING TO FLIP on the direct path, but the barrier is not optional:
  ; the payload may be running with the D-cache on (SvcMmuState answers
  ; that question) and the display controller is reading DRAM, not the
  ; cache. DisplayCacheLine cleans the surface's lines the same way the
  ; monitor's own console does after a CPU-drawn frame.
  If DisplayReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  DisplayCacheLine()
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwConRect(x, y, w, h, argb) - a filled, alpha-blended rectangle.
;
;  THE ONE PRIMITIVE THE WHOLE UI IS BUILT FROM. The V3D study measured
;  that GlIdeShell makes zero direct draw calls and reduces to a blended
;  rect, a tinted textured quad and a clip; the ELD's screens are the
;  same shape.
;
;  THE CALLER HAS ALREADY CLIPPED. Anvil/Hal/abi.pbi's SvcRect intersects
;  against the scissor and against the screen before calling here, because
;  rectangle arithmetic is not silicon knowledge and belongs where it can
;  be tested without a board. This procedure still refuses a range it
;  cannot draw - a second check on the one path that would otherwise write
;  outside the surface, which is the class of bug that corrupts something
;  else entirely and is diagnosed hours later.
;
;  ALPHA IS THE TOP BYTE. 255 is opaque and takes the fast path with no
;  read at all; 0 draws nothing; anything between reads the destination
;  and blends per channel. Blending in integers, 0..255, with the
;  round-to-nearest term - (a*s + (255-a)*d + 127) / 255 - because
;  dropping it darkens every translucent overlay by up to one level per
;  composite, and a UI composites the same panel every frame.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure HwConRect(x.i, y.i, w.i, h.i, argb.i)
  Define base.i
  Define pitch.i
  Define sw.i
  Define sh.i
  Define a.i
  Define sr.i
  Define sg.i
  Define sb.i
  Define row.i
  Define col.i
  Define p.i
  Define d.i
  Define dr.i
  Define dg.i
  Define db.i

  If DisplayReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  base = DisplayBase()
  If base = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If w <= 0 Or h <= 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  sw = DisplayWidth()
  sh = DisplayHeight()
  If x < 0
    w = w + x
    x = 0
  EndIf
  If y < 0
    h = h + y
    y = 0
  EndIf
  If x + w > sw
    w = sw - x
  EndIf
  If y + h > sh
    h = sh - y
  EndIf
  If w <= 0 Or h <= 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  a = (argb >> 24) & $FF
  If a = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  sr = (argb >> 16) & $FF
  sg = (argb >> 8) & $FF
  sb = argb & $FF

  pitch = DisplayPitch()
  row = 0
  While row < h
    p = base + (y + row) * pitch + x * 4
    col = 0
    While col < w
      If a = 255
        PokeL(p, argb & $00FFFFFF)
      Else
        d = PeekL(p)
        dr = (d >> 16) & $FF
        dg = (d >> 8) & $FF
        db = d & $FF
        dr = (a * sr + (255 - a) * dr + 127) / 255
        dg = (a * sg + (255 - a) * dg + 127) / 255
        db = (a * sb + (255 - a) * db + 127) / 255
        PokeL(p, (dr << 16) | (dg << 8) | db)
      EndIf
      p = p + 4
      col = col + 1
    Wend
    row = row + 1
  Wend
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwConText(x, y, *utf8, argb, bg) - one run of text, baseline at y.
;
;  y IS THE BASELINE AND NOT THE TOP, because that is what the rasteriser
;  underneath takes and because a UI that aligns two runs of different
;  glyphs aligns their baselines. HwConTextHeight() and the font's ascent
;  are what a caller converts a top-left box into a baseline with.
;
;  THE BACKGROUND IS A PARAMETER AND NOT AN ALPHA. DrawSmoothText blends
;  each glyph's coverage between fg and bg without reading the surface,
;  which is what makes it fast and what makes it wrong over a picture. For
;  the ELD's screens - flat panels of colour - passing the panel's own
;  colour is exactly right, and it is the caller who knows what that is.
;
;  Returns the width drawn.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwConText(x.i, y.i, *utf8, argb.i, bg.i)
  If DisplayReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If *utf8 = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn DrawSmoothText(x, y, *utf8, 0, argb & $00FFFFFF, bg & $00FFFFFF)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwConTextWidth(*utf8)
  If *utf8 = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn SmoothTextWidth(*utf8, 0)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwConBacklight(pct)
;
;  -1, AND THAT IS THE HONEST ANSWER RATHER THAN A NO-OP THAT RETURNS 0.
;  An HDMI monitor's brightness is the monitor's own, reachable only over
;  DDC/CI and not at all through this board's framebuffer path. A payload
;  that dimmed the screen for a night-driving mode and got a silent
;  success would show the driver a screen that never dims and no reason.
;  The DSI panel has a real backlight over the mailbox, and when that
;  backend exists it answers here.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwConBacklight(pct.i)
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure
